---
import QuestionLayout from '@layouts/QuestionLayout.astro';

const relatedLinks = [];
const _body = "<p>Your first capability runs in about five minutes if Rust and the <code>wasm32-wasip1</code> target are already installed. The CLI quickstart walks you through scaffolding a capability package, filling in its contract, compiling it, and running it with a JSON input.</p>\n\n      <h2>The five-minute path</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Install the CLI: <code>cargo install traverse-cli-rs</code> (installs the <code>traverse-cli</code> binary)</li>\n        <li>Scaffold a capability: <code>traverse-cli capability new your.namespace.your-cap</code> — this generates a <code>contract.json</code> with input/output JSON schemas plus a <code>#![no_std]</code> WASI guest stub</li>\n        <li>Fill in the schemas and the guest logic, then compile to <code>wasm32-wasip1</code></li>\n        <li>Validate the package with <code>traverse-cli capability-package inspect</code></li>\n        <li>Run it with <code>traverse-cli capability-package execute</code></li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>That loop is deliberately short. You do not need to understand everything to get output from your first run.</p>\n\n      <h2>The learning curve after that</h2>\n      <p>Getting comfortable with contracts takes a bit longer. You need to understand what a governing spec is, how input/output JSON Schema validation actually gates a call (preconditions and postconditions, by contrast, are documentation for reviewers today rather than something the runtime evaluates), and how a bundle groups capabilities together for registration. Most developers with Rust experience get there in an afternoon.</p>\n\n      <p>If you are new to Rust, budget more time. You do not need to be a Rust expert, but you do need to compile to WASM. The <a href=\"/questions/do-i-need-rust.html\">do I need Rust page</a> covers what level of Rust knowledge is actually required.</p>\n\n      <h2>Production readiness</h2>\n      <p>Getting a capability into production on the local target is straightforward. Integrating with AI agents via MCP, deploying to edge or cloud targets, and setting up a shared registry for a team takes more planning. The <a href=\"/docs/quickstart.html\">quickstart</a> is the right place to start either way.</p>";
const _jsonLd = "{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"FAQPage\",\"mainEntity\":[{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"How long does it take to get started with Traverse?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Your first capability takes about five minutes if you have Rust and the wasm32-wasip1 target installed. Scaffolding a capability with the CLI, filling in its contract, compiling the WASM binary, and running it through capability-package execute is a short loop. Production use takes longer — you need to understand contracts, bundles, placement targets, and how the registry fits into your deployment.\"}}]}";
---
<QuestionLayout
  title={"How long does it take to get started with Traverse?"}
  description={"Your first Traverse capability takes about five minutes. Learn what you need and what the learning curve looks like."}
  canonical={"https://traverse-framework.com/questions/how-long-to-get-started.html"}
  category={"Getting Started"}
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={_jsonLd}
>
  <Fragment set:html={_body} />
</QuestionLayout>
